---
/**
 * REVIEW ONLY, strip with the ?grain, ?tile, ?theme and ?tune switches in Layout.astro before
 * this lands on main.
 *
 * Links first. The sliders do the work now, so the ladder of fixed settings this page used to
 * list is gone: it made Hugo read a page per number, and the two sheets want different numbers
 * on different themes, which is four ladders rather than one.
 */

const THEMES = [
  { id: 'light', name: 'Light', page: '0.22', sheet: '0.36', blend: 'multiply' },
  { id: 'arctic', name: 'Arctic', page: '0.22', sheet: '0.36', blend: 'multiply' },
  { id: 'dark', name: 'Dark', page: '0.26', sheet: '0.38', blend: 'screen on an inverted tile' },
  { id: 'dark-forest', name: 'Forest', page: '0.24', sheet: '0.35', blend: 'screen on an inverted tile' },
] as const;

const TILES = [
  {
    query: '?tune&tile=lifted',
    name: 'The lifted tile',
    say: 'The same fibre centred on 232 rather than 216.4, so multiply darkens a light sheet less for the same spread.',
  },
  {
    query: '?tune&tile=patch-b',
    name: 'A different patch',
    say: 'Another crop of the same scan, coarser by half a level. The shipped one is the flattest of the 1,692 patches that size.',
  },
] as const;
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta name="robots" content="noindex" />
    <title>Paper grain, by hand</title>
    <style is:inline>
      :root {
        color-scheme: light dark;
        --ink: #1b1a17;
        --paper: #f6f3ee;
        --faint: #6b6560;
        --rule: #d8d1c7;
      }

      @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
        :root {
          --ink: #ece7df;
          --paper: #171614;
          --faint: #9a938a;
          --rule: #35322d;
        }
      }

      body {
        margin: 0 auto;
        padding: 2rem 1rem 6rem;
        max-width: 54rem;
        background: var(--paper);
        color: var(--ink);
        font: 1rem/1.5 system-ui, sans-serif;
      }

      h1 {
        font-size: 1.5rem;
        margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
      }

      h2 {
        font-size: 1.125rem;
        margin: 2.25rem 0 0.25rem;
        padding-top: 0.75rem;
        border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
      }

      p {
        margin: 0.25rem 0 0.5rem;
        color: var(--faint);
      }

      ul {
        margin: 0.5rem 0 0;
        padding: 0;
        list-style: none;
      }

      li {
        margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
      }

      li a {
        font-weight: 600;
      }

      .say {
        color: var(--faint);
      }

      .scroll {
        overflow-x: auto;
      }

      table {
        width: 100%;
        margin: 0.75rem 0 0;
        border-collapse: collapse;
      }

      th,
      td {
        padding: 0.4rem 0.75rem 0.4rem 0;
        text-align: left;
        vertical-align: top;
        border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
      }

      th {
        font-weight: 600;
      }

      td.num,
      th.num {
        text-align: right;
        padding-right: 1rem;
        font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
        white-space: nowrap;
      }

      code {
        font-family: ui-monospace, monospace;
        font-size: 0.875em;
      }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <h1>Paper grain, by hand</h1>
    <p>
      Each link opens the CV with two sliders on it: one for a drawing stack's sheets, one for the
      contributions paper. They move the live page, they remember a number per theme, and the
      panel hands the four themes back as a table to paste into the pull request.
    </p>

    <h2>Open the sliders</h2>
    <ul>
      {THEMES.map((theme) => (
        <li>
          <a href={`../?tune&theme=${theme.id}`}>{theme.name}</a>{' '}
          <span class="say">
            Sheets {theme.page}, contributions {theme.sheet} on the branch. {theme.blend}.
          </span>
        </li>
      ))}
      <li>
        <a href="../?tune">Whatever theme you are on</a>{' '}
        <span class="say">The panel switches theme itself, so one link does for all four.</span>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The same sliders on the other two tiles</h2>
    <ul>
      {TILES.map((tile) => (
        <li>
          <a href={`../${tile.query}`}>{tile.name}</a> <span class="say">{tile.say}</span>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>

    <h2>How to hand the numbers back</h2>
    <p>
      Set a theme, move both sliders until the two sheets read the way you want, switch theme, do
      it again. Copy hands you the four rows below. Paste them into the pull request or the chat
      and they get baked into ThemePicker.
    </p>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>theme</th>
            <th class="num">drawing sheets</th>
            <th class="num">contributions</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {THEMES.map((theme) => (
            <tr>
              <td>
                <code>{theme.id}</code>
              </td>
              <td class="num">{theme.page}</td>
              <td class="num">{theme.sheet}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Those are what the branch paints today. On the light themes the contributions sheet reads
      L* 91.7 against 96.5 bare, a spread of 2.60 levels, and a drawing sheet spreads 1.32. The
      other three themes land within 0.15 levels of those. Nothing is forced: the mechanism is
      the same one the site ships, and the sliders only write the two numbers.
    </p>
  </body>
</html>
